I think I found one concrete cause of the "import does nothing" behavior for qBittorrent users.

**Root cause**

In the current Docker image (I tested `ghcr.io/doezer/questarr:latest` / v1.4.2), `dist/server/cron.js` builds the import path like this:

```js
const remoteImportPath = buildRemoteImportPath(
  details.downloadDir,
  details.name,
);
```

It uses the torrent's **display name** (`details.name`) instead of the actual file/folder name. For single-file torrents where qBittorrent's display name differs from the real filename — e.g. display name `Flotsam (v1.0.1f7)` but actual file `Flotsam.v1.0.1f7.rar` — Questarr tries to import `/downloads/Flotsam (v1.0.1f7)`, which does not exist. It retries until it silently gives up, so the import never happens.

`DownloaderManager.getDownloadDetails()` already returns the correct file list in `details.files`, so the needed information is available.

**Possible solution**

Use the existing `resolveDownloadRelativePath()` helper from the source repo (or equivalent logic): for a single-file torrent, use `details.files[0].name`; for multi-file torrents, keep using the torrent name as the folder. Then call:

```ts
const relativePath = resolveDownloadRelativePath(details);
const remoteImportPath = buildRemoteImportPath(
  details.downloadDir,
  relativePath,
);
```

In `server/cron.ts`, replace the direct `details.name` call with the two lines above.

I verified this by patching the compiled `cron.js` in a running container and importing a previously-failing torrent; the import succeeded.

_(AI was used for investigation and fix, and the workaround was tested on a local deployment.)_

Got it fixed using claude. He wrote a summary of the problem, the fix and a regression test to test this in the future. Here is his summary:

# Fix: `localhost` downloads fail with `Failed to resolve hostname: [::1]`

## Symptom

Adding a download (e.g. via SABnzbd) fails with:

```
All downloaders failed. Errors: SABnzbd: Failed to add NZB to SABnzbd: Failed to resolve hostname: [::1]
```

This happens even though the downloader/indexer is configured with `localhost` and
**Test Connection succeeds**.

## Root cause

The bug lives in `server/ssrf.ts`, in the two SSRF helpers. They handled
IPv6-**literal** hostnames inconsistently:

| URL                   | `isSafeUrl(...)` (the guard)                      | `safeFetch(...)` (the request)                    |
| --------------------- | ------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------- |
| `http://[::1]:8080/…` | ✅ `true` — strips `[ ]`, validates `::1` as safe | ❌ **throws** `Failed to resolve hostname: [::1]` |

`URL.hostname` keeps the surrounding brackets for IPv6 literals, so it returns the
string `"[::1]"` (with brackets). In `safeFetch`:

- `isIP("[::1]")` returns `0` (the brackets make it an invalid IP literal), so
- it falls through to `dns.lookup("[::1]")`, which fails →
  `throw new Error("Failed to resolve hostname: [::1]")`.

`isSafeUrl` already stripped the brackets before validating, so the URL **passed the
safety guard** (e.g. `isSafeUrl(request.url)` in `server/downloaders.ts`) and only
blew up later inside `safeFetch`.

### Why `localhost` triggers it

- Node 22 resolves `localhost` **IPv6-first** (`::1` before `127.0.0.1`, default
  `dns` result order is `verbatim`).
- The NZB/torrent download link coming back from the indexer (e.g. Prowlarr on
  `localhost:9696`) ends up in the bracketed IPv6 literal form `http://[::1]:…/`.
- **Test Connection** uses a plain `fetch` (which tolerates `[::1]`), which is why
  the test passes while the actual download — routed through `safeFetch` — fails.

## The fix

In `server/ssrf.ts`, strip IPv6 brackets at the top of `safeFetch`, mirroring the
bracket handling that already exists in `isSafeUrl`:

```ts
const url = new URL(urlStr);
// URL.hostname keeps the surrounding brackets for IPv6 literals (e.g. "[::1]"),
// but isIP() and dns.lookup() expect the bare address. Strip them so IPv6
// literal hosts are validated directly instead of failing DNS resolution.
// This mirrors the bracket handling in isSafeUrl above.
let hostname = url.hostname;
if (hostname.startsWith("[") && hostname.endsWith("]")) {
  hostname = hostname.slice(1, -1);
}
```

(Change `const hostname` → `let hostname` and add the bracket strip.)

After stripping, `::1` is correctly recognized by `isIP` as an IPv6 literal, so it
skips DNS entirely, is validated directly as a safe loopback address, and is
re-bracketed for the outbound request by the existing HTTP-rewrite line:

```ts
safeUrl.hostname = family === 6 ? `[${address}]` : address;
```

## Regression test

Added to `server/__tests__/ssrf.test.ts` (in the `safeFetch` `describe` block, where
`dns.lookup` and `fetch` are already mocked):

```ts
it("should connect to IPv6 literal hosts without DNS resolution (e.g. [::1])", async () => {
  // URL.hostname keeps the brackets ("[::1]"). safeFetch must strip them so the
  // address is treated as an IP literal instead of being sent to dns.lookup,
  // which would fail with "Failed to resolve hostname: [::1]".
  vi.mocked(fetch).mockResolvedValueOnce(new Response("ok"));

  await safeFetch("http://[::1]:8080/api");

  // Should not attempt DNS resolution for an IP literal
  expect(dns.lookup).not.toHaveBeenCalled();
  // For HTTP, should rewrite to the bracketed IPv6 address
  expect(fetch).toHaveBeenCalledWith(
    "http://[::1]:8080/api",
    expect.objectContaining({ headers: expect.any(Headers) })
  );
});
```

Run it with:

```bash
npx vitest run server/__tests__/ssrf.test.ts
```

## How to verify against a live container

```bash
# Reproduce the failure (pre-fix): throws "Failed to resolve hostname: [::1]"
# while isSafeUrl returns true — proving the guard/fetch mismatch.
node --input-type=module -e '
import { safeFetch, isSafeUrl } from "/app/dist/server/ssrf.js";
const u = "http://[::1]:9696/2/api?mode=version";
console.log("isSafeUrl:", await isSafeUrl(u));
try { const r = await safeFetch(u); console.log("status:", r.status); }
catch (e) { console.log("THROWN:", e.message); }
'
```

Post-fix, `safeFetch` connects (returns an HTTP status) instead of throwing, and
`localhost`-configured downloads succeed end to end.

issue.body:
Summary
Every download attempt fails with HTTP 500 — "All downloaders failed: qBittorrent: Failed to download torrent file: Failed to download torrent: 500 Internal Server Error", regardless of indexer or game. Prowlarr logs show [Fatal] BadRequestException: Failed to normalize provided link on every GET /{N}/download call from Questarr.


Environment
Questarr (any branch including main and feat/629/add-vite-base-support) since PR #487 (commit 04baf42, Apr 5 2026).
Prowlarr v2.3.5.5327 with UrlBase=/torrent/prowlarr.
qBittorrent as the only configured download client.
Indexers stored in Questarr with hostname localhost.
Node 18+ runtime (default fetch via undici).
What's happening
server/torznab.ts (introduced in PR #487) rewrites the search-result link as follows:

If linkUrl.host !== indexerUrlObj.host, AND the indexer URL matches /{numericId}/api, Questarr wraps the link into a new Prowlarr proxy URL: http://{indexerHost}/{N}/download?link=<base64(originalLink)>&apikey=[redacted]&file=....
This is correct when the indexer returns a raw external (e.g. Cloudflare-fronted) URL.
It is wrong when the indexer is Prowlarr and the link already points to Prowlarr's own download proxy — but the host string differs because Node's fetch resolves localhost → 127.0.0.1 and Prowlarr echoes the resolved hostname in <link> elements.
Result: Questarr sends Prowlarr a /{N}/download?link=<base64(http://127.0.0.1:.../torrent/prowlarr/{N}/download?link=<base64(realIndexerUrl)>)>. Prowlarr's NewznabController.GetDownload calls normalizeLink, which rejects URLs pointing back to Prowlarr itself with BadRequestException: Failed to normalize provided link → 500.


Reproduction
Configure a Prowlarr instance with UrlBase set (e.g. behind a reverse proxy).
Add it to Questarr; indexer URLs are stored as http://localhost:<port>/{N}/api.
Trigger any download. Observe POST /api/downloads returns 500.
Prowlarr logs: [Fatal] ProwlarrErrorPipeline: Request Failed. GET /{N}/download followed by NzbDrone.Core.Exceptions.BadRequestException: Failed to normalize provided link.
Root cause is the host-mismatch check; can be reproduced without UrlBase by configuring the indexer URL with one hostname (localhost) and ensuring Node fetch resolves to a different literal (127.0.0.1). Newer Node default DNS order (ipv4first → verbatim) changed when this triggers.


Suggested fix
In the host-mismatch branch of server/torznab.ts, also check whether the link path is already a Prowlarr download proxy URL (/\d+/download/?$). If so, the mismatch is just hostname aliasing — perform a plain host rewrite instead of wrapping. Only wrap when the link is a genuinely external URL.

Minimal patch:

if (linkUrl.host !== indexerUrlObj.host) {
  const linkIsAlreadyProwlarrProxy = /\/\d+\/download\/?$/.test(linkUrl.pathname);
  const prowlarrMatch = indexerUrlObj.pathname.match(/^\/(\d+)\/api\/?$/i);

  if (prowlarrMatch && indexer?.apiKey && !linkIsAlreadyProwlarrProxy) {
    // ... existing wrap logic
  } else {
    // host-swap only
    linkUrl.protocol = indexerUrlObj.protocol;
    linkUrl.host = indexerUrlObj.host;
    torznabItem.link = linkUrl.toString();
  }
}


Severity
Hard blocker: 100% of downloads fail for any user whose Prowlarr is on the same host as Questarr (typical bare-metal deployment). Worked before PR #487; broke silently after.

## Problem

Questarr is built with Vite's default `base: "/"`, so the served `index.html` references assets at absolute root paths (`/assets/*.js`, `/Questarr.svg`, etc.). The React code also hardcodes `/api/...` in `fetch` calls and `socket.io-client` connects at the default `/socket.io/` path on the page origin's root. The Express server mounts everything at `/`.

This means Questarr can only be hosted at the **root** of a domain. Mounting it under a subpath via a reverse proxy (e.g. `https://example.com/questarr/`) breaks immediately:

- The browser loads `/questarr/` and gets the Vite-built `index.html` (200 OK).
- It then requests `/assets/index-xxx.js` at the **site root** — this falls through whatever reverse-proxy `location` block was set up for `/questarr/` and either 404s or, more confusingly, hits another app on the same host and returns its `index.html` with `Content-Type: text/html`.
- With `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`, Firefox raises `NS_ERROR_CORRUPTED_CONTENT` for every JS/CSS request and the page renders blank.

This is a common scenario for self-hosted setups where a single domain fronts many services (Sonarr/Radarr/Prowlarr/Bazarr/qBittorrent etc. all support being mounted under a subpath for exactly this reason).

## Reproduction

1. Run Questarr on `127.0.0.1:5000`.
2. Add an nginx block on a different host:
   ```nginx
   location /questarr/ {
       proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:5000/;
       proxy_http_version 1.1;
       proxy_set_header Upgrade $http_upgrade;
       proxy_set_header Connection "upgrade";
   }

UNRAID 

Got it installed. Got persistent paths so Questarr sees qbittorrent.

Downloader connection client successful. 

