`AddGameModal` was calling the browser's raw `fetch()` for `/api/igdb/search`, bypassing the `apiFetch()` wrapper that applies `withBasePath()`. This caused 404s in subdir-app-url deployments where `QUESTARR_BASE_PATH` is configured.

## Changes

- **`client/src/components/AddGameModal.tsx`**: Replace raw `fetch()` with `apiFetch()` (already imported) for the IGDB search query — this ensures `withBasePath()` is applied and the Authorization header is injected automatically, removing the manual token construction.

```diff
- const token = localStorage.getItem("token");
- const headers: Record<string, string> = {};
- if (token) {
-   headers["Authorization"] = `******;
- }
- const response = await fetch(`/api/igdb/search?${params.toString()}`, { headers });
+ const response = await apiFetch(`/api/igdb/search?${params.toString()}`);
```

`ClaimDownloadModal` and `ClaimBatchModal` already use `apiRequest()` (which delegates to `apiFetch()`) and are unaffected.

<!-- START COPILOT CODING AGENT SUFFIX -->

- Fixes #925

`SYNO.DownloadStation2.Task.create` requires an explicit `type` field (`"torrent"` or `"nzb"`) in the multipart body when creating a task via file upload. Without it the API rejects the request with error 120 (unknown file type), making every torrent/NZB download silently fail after authentication succeeds.

## Changes

- **`server/downloaders/synology.ts`** — append `type` to the `FormData` before the upload call, only when the DS2 API is selected (the legacy `SYNO.DownloadStation.Task` API infers the type from the file and doesn't need it):

```ts
if (apiName === "SYNO.DownloadStation2.Task") {
  formData.append("type", request.downloadType === "usenet" ? "nzb" : "torrent");
}
```

- **`server/__tests__/downloaders_clients_regression.test.ts`** — extend the existing DS2 file-upload test to assert the `type` field is present and correct in the submitted `FormData`.

The previous branch targeted the wrong baseline: `release/1.4.0` already contained Synology Download Station support, but URL/magnet submissions were failing at task creation (`SYNO.DownloadStation2.Task.create ... code 120`). This change focuses on correcting that existing implementation rather than reintroducing the downloader.

- **Synology Task.create compatibility**
  - Simplified URL/magnet submission payload to use the API-compatible `uri` field (with optional `destination`) for task creation.
  - Removed the DS2-specific `type/url/create_list` parameter set that led to runtime rejection on affected Synology instances.

- **Regression coverage**
  - Added a focused assertion in Synology downloader tests to lock the `requestTaskApi("create", ...)` payload shape for redirected magnet flows.

```ts
await this.requestTaskApi<SynologyTaskResponse>("create", {
  httpMethod: "POST",
  params: {
    uri: downloadUrl,
    destination,
  },
});
```

Questarr started failing all torrent downloads after the Torznab rewrite logic began re-wrapping Prowlarr download links. When Prowlarr returned its own proxy URL but the hostname differed by alias (`localhost` vs `127.0.0.1`), Questarr produced a doubly wrapped `link` parameter that Prowlarr rejected.

- **Torznab rewrite guard for existing Prowlarr proxy links**
  - In `server/torznab.ts`, for Prowlarr-style indexers (`/{id}/api`):
    - If incoming link is already a Prowlarr proxy URL (`/{id}/download` with `link` query param), Questarr now rewrites only protocol/host to the configured indexer URL.
    - Questarr no longer rebuilds a new proxy URL in this case, preventing nested base64 encoding.
  - Existing behavior remains for true external/raw links: Questarr still constructs a Prowlarr proxy download URL.

- **Regression coverage for hostname alias case**
  - Added test in `server/__tests__/torznab.test.ts` to cover:
    - Configured indexer host: `localhost`
    - Returned link host: `127.0.0.1`
    - Expected: rewritten host with preserved original `link` payload (no double-wrap).

```ts
const isProwlarrProxyUrl =
  /^\/\d+\/download\/?$/i.test(linkUrl.pathname) && linkUrl.searchParams.has("link");

if (isProwlarrProxyUrl) {
  linkUrl.protocol = indexerUrlObj.protocol;
  linkUrl.host = indexerUrlObj.host;
  torznabItem.link = linkUrl.toString();
}
```

Questarr was sending broken torrent and magnet links to download clients (qBittorrent, Transmission, rTorrent) when downloading via Prowlarr. Prowlarr wraps external torrent URLs in a proxy URL whose `link` query parameter is base64-encoded. That base64 can contain literal `+` characters. When qBittorrent (or Questarr in fallback mode) fetches the Prowlarr URL, ASP.NET Core (Prowlarr's backend) decodes `+` as space — corrupting the base64 and causing Prowlarr to redirect to a wrong or broken torrent/magnet link. The same magnet added manually always succeeded because qBittorrent was fetching the correct content directly, bypassing the encoding issue.

This is the same class of bug fixed for SABnzbd (`fixNzbUrlEncoding`), now applied consistently across all torrent download clients.

## Changes

- **`server/downloaders.ts`**:
  - Applied `fixNzbUrlEncoding` inside `fetchWithMagnetDetection` so that every client using this function (Transmission, rTorrent, qBittorrent fallback) re-encodes literal `+` as `%2B` before fetching from any indexer/Prowlarr URL
  - Applied `fixNzbUrlEncoding` to qBittorrent's step-1 URL-based add for non-magnet URLs, so the URL qBittorrent itself fetches from Prowlarr is also correctly encoded (magnet links are unchanged — no-op)
  - Updated the `fixNzbUrlEncoding` comment to explicitly note it applies to Torznab (torrent) URLs as well as NZB/Newznab

```ts
// fetchWithMagnetDetection — now fixes encoding before any fetch
let currentUrl = fixNzbUrlEncoding(url);

// qBittorrent step-1 URL-based add — fixed URL passed to qBittorrent
const urlToAdd = isMagnet ? request.url : fixNzbUrlEncoding(request.url);
params.set("urls", urlToAdd);
```

- **`server/__tests__/magnet_detection.test.ts`** — updated the `+` encoding test to verify the new behaviour: `fixNzbUrlEncoding` converts `+` to `%2B` before the first fetch, so the corrected URL (not the original) is what reaches the indexer server

When a download URL redirects to a `magnet:` URI, `QBittorrentClient` recursively calls `addDownload` with the magnet URL. The initial `isSafeUrl` guard then rejects it — magnet URIs don't start with `http(s)://`, so the function prepended `http://`, producing `http://magnet:?xt=urn:btih:...`, which fails URL parsing or DNS resolution and returns `false`.

## Changes

- **`server/ssrf.ts`** — Early-return `true` in `isSafeUrl` for any `magnet:` URI. Magnet links carry no SSRF risk: they are never fetched server-side, only forwarded to the download client.

```typescript
// Magnet links are not HTTP(S) URLs and do not cause any server-side network connection.
// They are passed directly to the download client, so they pose no SSRF risk.
if (urlStr.startsWith("magnet:")) {
  return true;
}
```

- **`server/__tests__/ssrf.test.ts`** — Added test asserting `isSafeUrl` returns `true` for magnet URIs and that no DNS lookup is attempted.

The same `isSafeUrl` call also gates magnet links in `TransmissionClient`, so both clients benefit from this fix.

